<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Your questions, answered</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Who sees me?</summary>
      <p>Aircraft fitted with ADS-L. skyBlip Go also receives FLARM, but transmits only ADS-L.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I see airliners?</summary>
      <p>
        Not with a Go alone. Airliners transmit ADS-B on 1090&nbsp;MHz, which a
        Go does not receive. skyBlip Bravo will receive it, alone with your
        phone or paired with your Go as a Tango, once its firmware is released:
        <%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "skyBlip Bravo" %>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Will Bravo see every aircraft with a transponder?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Bravo will receive ADS-B, which a Mode S transponder sends only if
        it has ADS-B out, also called Extended Squitter. A transponder without
        it stays invisible to Bravo, as to any ADS-B receiver.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Does Bravo make me visible?</summary>
      <p>
        No. Bravo will receive only. Your position goes out in ADS-L from a Go,
        alone or paired with a Bravo as a Tango.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>I fly a glider with FLARM. What does skyBlip add?</summary>
      <p>
        A Go broadcasts your position in ADS-L, for the aircraft that receive
        ADS-L. It does not transmit FLARM and does not replace your FLARM unit.
        If your FLARM unit already shows ADS-B traffic, check what a Bravo
        would add before you buy one.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there a subscription?</summary>
      <p>No. Once you own a Go, everything works: transmitting, receiving, the alarm, updates. The firmware is free, and Bravo&rsquo;s, in development, will be too.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is it certified?</summary>
      <p>
        No. It is an aid to lookout. It replaces neither the lookout nor the
        transponder where one is required.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a phone?</summary>
      <p>
        Not with a Go. The radar, the alarm and the settings are on the device.
        The phone is for seeing the traffic in your navigation app. A Bravo
        alone will need one, since it has no screen.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there another version?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes, skyBlip Go+, on a LilyGO T-Echo Plus: an alarm with buzzer and
        vibration, a bigger battery.
        <%= link_to_page "buy", label: "Compare", anchor: "compare" %>.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if I want to go back?</summary>
      <p>
        The skyBlip firmware leaves the T-Echo&rsquo;s factory bootloader
        alone. The other software available for the device installs again as
        before.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">ADS-L, <span class="highlight">the common language</span> of everything that flies</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    The European electronic conspicuity standard, published by EASA. It
    covers everything from light aircraft to skydivers, by way of
    microlights, gliders, gyroplanes, helicopters, balloons and paragliders,
    and tomorrow drones.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>Open.</h3>
      <p>A public specification that anyone can read and implement, and that skyBlip implements as open source.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Lightweight.</h3>
      <p>A frame of a few milliseconds, once a second, on the licence-free 868&nbsp;MHz band. A pocket device on a battery is enough.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Interoperable.</h3>
      <p>Its channels follow those of FLARM and PilotAware. skyBlip Go also receives FLARM traffic.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

